WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.300 --> 00:00:05.740
Helen Walters: Hello everyone.

00:00:05.780 --> 00:00:07.300
Happy 2024 wherever you are.

00:00:07.300 --> 00:00:10.740
It is January 8, and I don't know
about you, but here at TED,

00:00:10.780 --> 00:00:15.500
we are looking forward to the year ahead
with a sense of nervous anticipation.

00:00:15.500 --> 00:00:17.500
As we all know, there is a lot going on,

00:00:17.540 --> 00:00:20.580
from war on multiple fronts
to upcoming elections

00:00:20.620 --> 00:00:24.620
that some say will determine no less
than the future of democracy.

00:00:24.620 --> 00:00:26.340
This is big, this is concerning.

00:00:26.380 --> 00:00:28.420
It's often slightly confounding.

00:00:28.420 --> 00:00:31.740
So given all that, we want to move
forward with eyes wide open,

00:00:31.780 --> 00:00:36.700
and who better to help us understand
exactly what to pay attention to this year

00:00:36.740 --> 00:00:39.740
than president of Eurasia Group
and GZERO media, Ian Bremmer.

00:00:39.780 --> 00:00:40.940
Ian, hi,

00:00:40.940 --> 00:00:42.900
Ian Bremmer: Helen, good to be with you.

00:00:42.900 --> 00:00:47.420
HW: So, Ian, you've just published
your annual list of Top Risks for 2024,

00:00:47.420 --> 00:00:48.940
and I want to dive right in.

00:00:48.940 --> 00:00:54.020
The very first one that you describe is
called "the United States versus itself."

00:00:54.060 --> 00:00:55.700
So tell us.

00:00:55.740 --> 00:00:57.540
IB: Helen, the United States today

00:00:57.580 --> 00:01:00.900
has an incredibly strong
economy and military.

00:01:00.900 --> 00:01:04.420
Its political system is in crisis.

00:01:04.940 --> 00:01:08.220
The US is the only advanced
industrial democracy

00:01:08.260 --> 00:01:12.860
that cannot ensure a free
and fair transition of power

00:01:12.860 --> 00:01:16.220
that is seen as legitimate
by a majority of its population.

00:01:16.260 --> 00:01:20.300
That is what we are looking at in 2024,

00:01:20.300 --> 00:01:23.380
and it's happening against a context

00:01:23.420 --> 00:01:27.980
of a geopolitical environment
that is very deeply unstable,

00:01:28.020 --> 00:01:30.100
with a major war between
Russia and Ukraine,

00:01:30.140 --> 00:01:31.740
which is nowhere close to ending,

00:01:31.740 --> 00:01:34.740
with a major war between Israel and Hamas,

00:01:34.740 --> 00:01:36.740
which is nowhere close to ending.

00:01:37.180 --> 00:01:41.860
And so both allies of the United States
are deeply concerned about this,

00:01:41.900 --> 00:01:45.420
and adversaries are looking
to take advantage.

00:01:45.460 --> 00:01:47.460
Now for 2024,

00:01:47.500 --> 00:01:48.980
we won't have a new president

00:01:48.980 --> 00:01:51.140
if there is a new president,
that's next year.

00:01:51.180 --> 00:01:52.460
So why is it so risky now?

00:01:52.500 --> 00:01:56.180
Well, it's so risky now
because the country is so divided

00:01:56.220 --> 00:01:58.780
and because Trump is likely
to get the nomination

00:01:58.780 --> 00:02:01.460
overwhelmingly likely, and when he does,

00:02:01.500 --> 00:02:04.300
his policy pronouncements
will drive the GOP.

00:02:04.340 --> 00:02:06.940
And they are not right now, as of today.

00:02:06.980 --> 00:02:12.060
So in other words, overnight,
he will gain the loyalty,

00:02:12.100 --> 00:02:16.300
regain the loyalty of the overwhelming
majority of Republican leaders

00:02:16.340 --> 00:02:20.820
in state legislatures,
in the House, in the Senate,

00:02:20.860 --> 00:02:26.300
and of Republican-leaning
and right-leaning media outlets

00:02:26.340 --> 00:02:30.220
and, of course, the ability to raise money
and deploy that money for the election.

00:02:30.260 --> 00:02:32.740
And that means
that his policy orientations,

00:02:32.780 --> 00:02:34.220
as he expresses them,

00:02:34.220 --> 00:02:37.260
whether it's cutting off Zelensky
and the Ukrainians

00:02:37.300 --> 00:02:39.500
or showing the Iranians what's what,

00:02:39.540 --> 00:02:42.300
and that's why they wouldn't
have gone to war

00:02:42.300 --> 00:02:45.100
against Israel and the US
if he had been president,

00:02:45.140 --> 00:02:46.340
unlike Biden.

00:02:46.380 --> 00:02:48.260
Or in terms of the border,

00:02:48.300 --> 00:02:51.940
vis a vis the Mexicans,
any other issues,

00:02:51.980 --> 00:02:58.220
those are suddenly going to be drivers
of one half of the US political system.

00:02:58.260 --> 00:03:02.300
So it's a deeply concerning
political environment,

00:03:02.340 --> 00:03:07.860
and one that the United States is not
in a position to respond to effectively.

00:03:07.860 --> 00:03:09.180
HW: So, Ian, you've written

00:03:09.180 --> 00:03:11.580
that there is an unlikely
but plausible possibility

00:03:11.580 --> 00:03:15.180
that the US won't actually even be able
to hold a free and fair election

00:03:15.220 --> 00:03:16.660
on November 5.

00:03:16.700 --> 00:03:17.860
We're going to talk more

00:03:17.860 --> 00:03:20.700
about the impact of artificial
intelligence in a little bit,

00:03:20.700 --> 00:03:23.780
but the reality is that we have seen
the impact of misinformation

00:03:23.780 --> 00:03:24.980
on elections before.

00:03:24.980 --> 00:03:27.660
But in the ensuing time,
things have gotten much [worse],

00:03:27.700 --> 00:03:28.860
much quicker.

00:03:28.900 --> 00:03:30.820
So what should we be watching for there,

00:03:30.820 --> 00:03:34.100
and how do you think that is going
to play out in November in the US?

00:03:34.140 --> 00:03:38.580
IB: Well, the United States,
as the most powerful country in the world

00:03:38.580 --> 00:03:41.620
and as a political democracy,
which is in crisis,

00:03:41.620 --> 00:03:44.580
the most vulnerable part
of the United States

00:03:44.620 --> 00:03:46.220
is its political system

00:03:46.220 --> 00:03:49.780
and is specifically its 2024 election.

00:03:49.820 --> 00:03:52.460
That is the Achilles heel
for the United States.

00:03:52.500 --> 00:03:57.060
When I speak with the intelligence
leaders in the United States,

00:03:57.100 --> 00:04:00.100
they say that is what
they are most concerned about.

00:04:00.100 --> 00:04:02.580
It's the vulnerability of the US election.

00:04:02.580 --> 00:04:05.700
It's not the Russians attacking
the Americans militarily,

00:04:05.700 --> 00:04:07.900
it's not a big fight with the Chinese,

00:04:07.900 --> 00:04:09.100
it's not Iran,

00:04:09.140 --> 00:04:11.500
it's the vulnerability
of the US elections.

00:04:11.540 --> 00:04:14.540
And that vulnerability
is a comparatively complex

00:04:14.580 --> 00:04:17.940
and soft target from a homeland
security perspective.

00:04:17.940 --> 00:04:22.900
Especially because Americans don't live
in the same information environment.

00:04:22.940 --> 00:04:25.620
You know, we talk about climate
change around the world,

00:04:25.620 --> 00:04:28.620
and everyone agrees
that there's 1.2 degrees of warming.

00:04:28.660 --> 00:04:32.420
Everyone agrees there's 442 parts
per million of carbon in the atmosphere.

00:04:32.460 --> 00:04:34.940
When you talk about the US
political system,

00:04:34.940 --> 00:04:37.140
everyone does not agree

00:04:37.180 --> 00:04:42.380
that Trump tried illegally
to overturn an election.

00:04:42.420 --> 00:04:46.820
I mean, in a normal democracy,
in a well-functioning democracy,

00:04:46.860 --> 00:04:51.700
that would obviously be
the top issue of debate

00:04:51.740 --> 00:04:52.940
in the election.

00:04:52.980 --> 00:04:55.700
Is the fact that you're thinking
about re-electing someone

00:04:55.740 --> 00:05:00.420
that is, you know, not interested
in a democratic election.

00:05:00.780 --> 00:05:03.700
That is not what is happening
presently in the United States.

00:05:03.740 --> 00:05:04.900
Not at all.

00:05:04.900 --> 00:05:06.500
And that is not Trump's fault.

00:05:06.540 --> 00:05:09.780
Trump is a symptom, a very serious symptom

00:05:09.820 --> 00:05:15.700
of the fact that US institutions
have been delegitimized over decades.

00:05:15.740 --> 00:05:17.140
And it's getting worse.

00:05:17.180 --> 00:05:22.020
And he's a very happy
beneficiary of that reality.

00:05:22.020 --> 00:05:24.980
So, yes, I do worry

00:05:25.020 --> 00:05:27.740
that American political institutions,

00:05:27.780 --> 00:05:31.180
and specifically, the electoral procedures
are vulnerable to that,

00:05:31.180 --> 00:05:35.460
especially because the stakes
are so much higher this time around.

00:05:35.500 --> 00:05:38.340
The stakes are higher
for Biden and his team

00:05:38.340 --> 00:05:44.020
because they believe
that they may face an end

00:05:44.020 --> 00:05:49.500
to an effective multi-party
transfer of power over time

00:05:49.540 --> 00:05:50.860
if Trump wins.

00:05:50.860 --> 00:05:54.540
A lot of them individually believe
that they would face legal jeopardy

00:05:54.540 --> 00:05:59.700
from a politicized Department
of Justice or FBI or IRS

00:05:59.740 --> 00:06:00.900
if Trump is in power.

00:06:00.900 --> 00:06:02.580
They absolutely say that privately.

00:06:02.620 --> 00:06:05.860
And of course, Trump believes, to himself,

00:06:05.900 --> 00:06:08.100
much more dangerous
than the end of democracy,

00:06:08.100 --> 00:06:11.140
Trump believes that he would face jail,

00:06:11.180 --> 00:06:14.660
he and maybe even
members of his family,

00:06:14.700 --> 00:06:18.260
if he were to lose the election.

00:06:18.780 --> 00:06:22.140
So the stakes are very high indeed
in the United States.

00:06:22.140 --> 00:06:24.940
They're very high indeed
outside the United States.

00:06:24.980 --> 00:06:28.540
HW: I think the reality that you're
describing is that whoever wins,

00:06:28.580 --> 00:06:32.420
whoever the nominees are, Biden, Trump,

00:06:32.460 --> 00:06:33.620
whoever wins,

00:06:33.620 --> 00:06:36.740
things aren't necessarily
going to get better, right?

00:06:37.220 --> 00:06:39.780
IB: Yeah, I guess I'm trying to say

00:06:39.780 --> 00:06:42.420
that neither Biden nor Trump

00:06:42.460 --> 00:06:46.220
have the capacity and the willingness

00:06:46.260 --> 00:06:48.100
to try to fix this.

00:06:48.540 --> 00:06:51.380
I don't believe that Trump is interested,

00:06:51.420 --> 00:06:53.180
never mind having the capacity.

00:06:53.220 --> 00:06:55.180
I believe that he benefits.

00:06:55.180 --> 00:07:00.940
He believes he benefits from referring
to his adversaries politically as enemies,

00:07:00.940 --> 00:07:02.700
as enemies of the people.

00:07:02.700 --> 00:07:06.300
And as painting them
as an existential threat

00:07:06.300 --> 00:07:09.020
to the Republic of the United States.

00:07:09.660 --> 00:07:12.140
You know, they're Marxists,
they're communists,

00:07:12.140 --> 00:07:14.620
it's a new McCarthyism, if you will.

00:07:15.500 --> 00:07:19.420
And that Biden

00:07:19.460 --> 00:07:22.620
certainly has the willingness

00:07:22.660 --> 00:07:26.740
to try to fix this yawning divide

00:07:26.780 --> 00:07:27.940
in the United States.

00:07:27.980 --> 00:07:32.020
But four years of being president
hasn't fixed it, right?

00:07:32.020 --> 00:07:35.100
I mean, there was a belief
among many Biden supporters

00:07:35.140 --> 00:07:37.100
that if you elected this guy,

00:07:37.140 --> 00:07:40.260
that the United States was going
to be able to become more normal,

00:07:40.300 --> 00:07:43.980
and Biden has had
a lot of legislative successes,

00:07:43.980 --> 00:07:47.420
his infrastructure package
that was bipartisan,

00:07:47.460 --> 00:07:48.900
that Trump was unable to pass,

00:07:48.940 --> 00:07:51.940
the Inflation Reduction Act, misnamed,

00:07:51.980 --> 00:07:54.900
but nonetheless significant
amounts of money

00:07:54.900 --> 00:07:57.780
for investment into red
and blue-state jobs,

00:07:57.820 --> 00:08:00.140
more red-state than blue-state jobs,

00:08:00.180 --> 00:08:01.740
other policies as well.

00:08:01.780 --> 00:08:05.340
And yet the divisions
in the United States,

00:08:05.380 --> 00:08:07.380
the dysfunction
of the US political system,

00:08:07.420 --> 00:08:12.580
the perceived illegitimacy of major
US political institutions and the media,

00:08:12.580 --> 00:08:16.340
has only increased
under four years of Biden.

00:08:16.340 --> 00:08:19.100
So you have this very unusual environment

00:08:19.140 --> 00:08:22.140
where the most powerful
leaders in the country

00:08:22.140 --> 00:08:25.140
are not able to fix the problem.

00:08:25.180 --> 00:08:29.820
And it's not like there's any diplomacy
that's happening between them.

00:08:29.820 --> 00:08:35.300
I mean, the interesting thing
about our view of the Top Risks of 2024

00:08:35.340 --> 00:08:37.980
is that you have these three
major conflicts,

00:08:37.980 --> 00:08:40.860
three major wars that are essentially
happening in the world.

00:08:40.900 --> 00:08:42.660
You've got, you know, Russia-Ukraine,

00:08:42.700 --> 00:08:44.460
which is now entering its third year,

00:08:44.500 --> 00:08:46.140
You've got Israel-Hamas,

00:08:46.140 --> 00:08:47.860
which is in its third month.

00:08:47.900 --> 00:08:50.180
And then you've got the US versus itself,

00:08:50.220 --> 00:08:52.180
which is kicking off right now.

00:08:52.180 --> 00:08:54.500
And in all three of those cases,

00:08:54.500 --> 00:08:58.900
there's no plausible environment
in the coming year

00:08:58.900 --> 00:09:03.300
where diplomacy is going
to reduce those tensions,

00:09:03.340 --> 00:09:07.420
is going to end or even
contain that conflict.

00:09:07.420 --> 00:09:10.180
And in all three of those wars,

00:09:10.220 --> 00:09:13.540
the leaders do not share
the same information space.

00:09:13.580 --> 00:09:16.420
They don't even agree
on the same basic set of facts.

00:09:16.460 --> 00:09:21.740
And that is an unprecedentedly
dangerous geopolitical environment

00:09:21.780 --> 00:09:23.580
in your and my lifetimes, Helen.

00:09:24.500 --> 00:09:27.060
HW: So let's talk about these
international conflicts.

00:09:27.100 --> 00:09:28.820
So you mentioned three of them,

00:09:28.860 --> 00:09:30.620
one, the United States versus itself.

00:09:30.660 --> 00:09:32.540
Let's turn to the Middle East.

00:09:32.580 --> 00:09:35.420
Let's talk about what's going on
with Israel-Hamas.

00:09:35.420 --> 00:09:39.220
Do you feel like this is going
to spiral into a broader conflict?

00:09:39.220 --> 00:09:40.900
IB: I do, I do.

00:09:41.500 --> 00:09:44.340
I'm not confident

00:09:44.380 --> 00:09:49.220
exactly what the avenue
of that escalation is likely to be,

00:09:49.260 --> 00:09:54.940
but I'm very confident there are so many
avenues that that escalation can occur.

00:09:54.980 --> 00:09:59.180
And it's not within the capacity
of the United States

00:09:59.180 --> 00:10:01.820
or other major countries around the world

00:10:01.860 --> 00:10:04.340
or in the region to contain it.

00:10:04.780 --> 00:10:06.580
And let's talk about a few.

00:10:07.260 --> 00:10:10.220
One, of course, is Hezbollah

00:10:10.260 --> 00:10:14.980
and the northern border
between Israel and Lebanon.

00:10:15.020 --> 00:10:18.900
And that fighting,
as we speak, is escalating.

00:10:18.940 --> 00:10:21.420
The missiles from Hezbollah into Israel,

00:10:21.460 --> 00:10:26.100
the Israeli assassination
of a Hamas political leader in Beirut,

00:10:26.100 --> 00:10:30.300
the willingness of the Israeli defense
forces to go against Hezbollah targets,

00:10:30.340 --> 00:10:31.940
That is escalating.

00:10:31.940 --> 00:10:34.460
And it's not just
Prime Minister Netanyahu,

00:10:34.460 --> 00:10:35.740
though he's very relevant,

00:10:35.740 --> 00:10:38.500
because if the war is over,
he's going to be out of power,

00:10:38.540 --> 00:10:40.140
and he could very well face jail,

00:10:40.140 --> 00:10:42.420
but it's also the entire
Israeli war cabinet

00:10:42.460 --> 00:10:44.740
that believes that they
cannot end this war

00:10:44.780 --> 00:10:47.940
and allow actors in the region

00:10:47.980 --> 00:10:50.580
that believe that Israel
has no right to exist,

00:10:50.620 --> 00:10:54.620
they can't allow them
to maintain power the way they did

00:10:54.660 --> 00:10:56.860
in the status quo ante before October 7.

00:10:56.900 --> 00:10:59.780
And that's not just "destroying" Hamas,

00:10:59.820 --> 00:11:02.820
however that is defined to be,

00:11:02.860 --> 00:11:04.620
but also,

00:11:04.660 --> 00:11:08.060
Hezbollah operating
right on the border

00:11:08.100 --> 00:11:12.580
in contravention of the UN
Security Council resolution.

00:11:12.580 --> 00:11:17.300
So they intend to back
Hezbollah off of that,

00:11:17.300 --> 00:11:21.740
to degrade their capacity
to attack Israel to a degree.

00:11:21.740 --> 00:11:24.620
There's also a willingness
of the Israelis to see that happen

00:11:24.660 --> 00:11:26.100
with the Houthis in Yemen,

00:11:26.140 --> 00:11:30.300
with Iraqi and Syrian radical Shia proxies

00:11:30.340 --> 00:11:32.140
of the Iranian government.

00:11:32.180 --> 00:11:36.060
The United States is now
increasingly actively fighting

00:11:36.100 --> 00:11:41.940
all of those actors in Yemen,
in Iraq and in Syria,

00:11:41.980 --> 00:11:46.900
and increasingly looks like
it might be willing to target Houthis

00:11:46.900 --> 00:11:49.380
in their Yemeni bases themselves.

00:11:49.420 --> 00:11:53.860
And that's even before we talk
about the radicalization

00:11:53.900 --> 00:11:57.220
of millions of Palestinians

00:11:57.220 --> 00:12:00.620
and their fellow Arab
and Muslim supporters

00:12:00.660 --> 00:12:01.820
all over the world

00:12:01.860 --> 00:12:05.780
on the back of the suffering
that they are experiencing,

00:12:05.820 --> 00:12:08.820
principally, but not only, in Gaza.

00:12:08.820 --> 00:12:13.740
Gaza is not livable right now
for two-plus million Palestinians,

00:12:13.780 --> 00:12:15.740
but they have nowhere to go.

00:12:15.780 --> 00:12:19.060
Those pressures are going to grow.

00:12:19.100 --> 00:12:23.540
The Israeli government
will be calling for their removal,

00:12:23.580 --> 00:12:25.900
into other territories like the Sinai,

00:12:25.900 --> 00:12:29.940
which will be seen to be ethnic cleansing
by almost everyone else,

00:12:29.980 --> 00:12:34.300
including the Biden administration
in the United States.

00:12:34.300 --> 00:12:38.860
That radicalization will lead
to lone-wolf violence

00:12:38.900 --> 00:12:40.060
and terrorist attacks,

00:12:40.100 --> 00:12:43.180
will lead to coordinated
terrorist attacks,

00:12:43.220 --> 00:12:44.460
not just in the region,

00:12:44.500 --> 00:12:46.980
but also in Europe,
also in the United States.

00:12:46.980 --> 00:12:50.260
So when you add all of that up,

00:12:50.260 --> 00:12:53.900
what you see is that it is much easier

00:12:53.900 --> 00:12:59.740
to understand how this conflict
will escalate substantially over 2024

00:12:59.780 --> 00:13:06.380
than threading a needle to see
how you might be able to contain it,

00:13:06.420 --> 00:13:10.060
largely to Israel versus Hamas

00:13:10.060 --> 00:13:14.700
in this tiny strip of land
that two-plus million Palestinians

00:13:14.740 --> 00:13:17.660
are trying to live on, in Gaza.

00:13:17.700 --> 00:13:20.900
That is where we are right now
in the Middle East.

00:13:20.940 --> 00:13:24.940
And this is deeply concerning
for the United States,

00:13:24.940 --> 00:13:28.980
because the US, in their support
of this Israeli war,

00:13:28.980 --> 00:13:32.140
is more isolated on the global stage

00:13:32.140 --> 00:13:35.180
than the Russians were,
even when they invaded Ukraine

00:13:35.220 --> 00:13:36.380
two years ago.

00:13:36.420 --> 00:13:38.580
And this also is a serious problem,

00:13:38.620 --> 00:13:41.300
not just for US projection
of power around the world,

00:13:41.340 --> 00:13:43.180
especially with the global South,

00:13:43.220 --> 00:13:45.700
but also domestically

00:13:45.740 --> 00:13:48.060
among Biden's Democratic supporters,

00:13:48.100 --> 00:13:52.540
a majority of whom are more inclined
to support the Palestinian cause

00:13:52.580 --> 00:13:54.300
than the Israeli cause.

00:13:54.340 --> 00:13:58.940
And so this is, as the conflict escalates,

00:13:58.980 --> 00:14:03.340
a proximate danger
to Biden's re-election efforts,

00:14:03.380 --> 00:14:08.140
which he is keenly aware of
but has very little he can do about it.

00:14:09.140 --> 00:14:12.220
HW: Right, the interconnections
and the tangled web

00:14:12.260 --> 00:14:15.740
becomes ever more tangled
as more actors get involved.

00:14:16.380 --> 00:14:19.660
This is a really like, a stupid,
deliberately stupid question.

00:14:19.700 --> 00:14:24.380
But do you feel like any hopes
for a two-state solution are vapor?

00:14:25.060 --> 00:14:28.380
IB: They're not vapor,
but they ain't close.

00:14:28.740 --> 00:14:35.100
I mean, I don't see,
in the foreseeable future, "security,"

00:14:35.140 --> 00:14:37.580
if I can use that term,

00:14:37.620 --> 00:14:43.060
will be provided by Israel
and not by anybody else.

00:14:43.500 --> 00:14:47.740
And you know, what that means
in the context of Gaza is hard to define.

00:14:47.780 --> 00:14:50.260
It's mostly about security
for the Israelis.

00:14:50.300 --> 00:14:53.140
It's not about security
for the Palestinians, but long-term,

00:14:53.180 --> 00:14:56.620
you can't have a two-state solution
unless you have effective governance

00:14:56.660 --> 00:15:00.700
that is seen as legitimate by Palestinians
living in Gaza and the West Bank,

00:15:00.740 --> 00:15:02.540
and the ability to defend themselves.

00:15:02.540 --> 00:15:06.180
The Israelis have made it very clear,
they have a right to defend themselves.

00:15:06.220 --> 00:15:09.180
And that has failed,

00:15:09.220 --> 00:15:13.140
in part because the Israeli government
was asleep at the switch

00:15:13.140 --> 00:15:14.580
before October 7.

00:15:14.620 --> 00:15:17.580
Israeli intelligence, defense forces
and, most importantly,

00:15:17.620 --> 00:15:20.780
their prime minister and government,
who had other priorities.

00:15:20.780 --> 00:15:25.060
And also because they are surrounded
by a number of organizations

00:15:25.060 --> 00:15:28.380
that do not recognize their right
to exist in the territory of Israel.

00:15:28.420 --> 00:15:29.660
So, absolutely,

00:15:29.700 --> 00:15:33.660
the Israelis have a right to ensure
the security of their people.

00:15:33.700 --> 00:15:35.940
But the Palestinians have that right, too.

00:15:35.980 --> 00:15:38.820
The difference is that the Israelis
not only have the right,

00:15:38.820 --> 00:15:40.780
they also have the capacity.

00:15:40.780 --> 00:15:43.060
They've got Iron Dome,

00:15:43.100 --> 00:15:45.220
provided in large part
by the United States,

00:15:45.260 --> 00:15:46.420
financed as well.

00:15:46.460 --> 00:15:49.540
They've got an incredible
asymmetrical military advantage,

00:15:49.580 --> 00:15:54.020
not only over the Palestinians,
but over everyone in the region.

00:15:54.620 --> 00:15:56.700
They only need to apply it effectively.

00:15:57.340 --> 00:16:02.420
And apply it effectively without killing
tens of thousands of civilians, right?

00:16:02.420 --> 00:16:08.180
While the Palestinians have
the right to self-defense,

00:16:08.220 --> 00:16:11.980
as is continually enshrined in votes
at the General Assembly

00:16:12.020 --> 00:16:14.740
by the vast majority of countries
from all over the world,

00:16:14.740 --> 00:16:16.180
including American allies,

00:16:16.220 --> 00:16:17.940
they just don't have the ability.

00:16:17.940 --> 00:16:21.260
And you can't talk
about a two-state solution

00:16:21.260 --> 00:16:23.540
until you can get the Palestinians

00:16:23.540 --> 00:16:26.780
the ability to govern themselves
and defend themselves.

00:16:26.780 --> 00:16:30.700
And we are very, very, very
far away from that.

00:16:30.740 --> 00:16:35.260
And since we're just talking
about 2024 today,

00:16:35.300 --> 00:16:39.820
there is no two-state solution
in the cards for 2024.

00:16:40.380 --> 00:16:43.460
As we think about a longer-
term environment,

00:16:43.460 --> 00:16:46.420
there will be no peace in the region

00:16:46.460 --> 00:16:49.820
until you find a plausible solution

00:16:49.860 --> 00:16:51.220
that is sustainable,

00:16:51.260 --> 00:16:54.420
where Palestinians feel
like they can raise their children

00:16:54.460 --> 00:16:56.980
with security and economic opportunity.

00:16:57.780 --> 00:17:01.460
We would all want only that for our kids,

00:17:01.460 --> 00:17:03.340
and they presently do not have that.

00:17:03.380 --> 00:17:05.740
They don't have anything
remotely close to that.

00:17:06.900 --> 00:17:10.780
HW: Alright, so one of the other risks
that you determine in the report

00:17:10.780 --> 00:17:12.980
is one of "partitioned Ukraine,"

00:17:13.020 --> 00:17:16.540
which is not a phrase that Ukrainians
are going to be excited about,

00:17:16.540 --> 00:17:20.180
but it's one that you actually think
is going to become a reality in 2024.

00:17:20.220 --> 00:17:21.860
So tell us what's going on here.

00:17:21.860 --> 00:17:25.020
IB: Yeah, I want to be clear
that writing about partitioned Ukraine

00:17:25.060 --> 00:17:27.100
is not a personal preference.

00:17:27.140 --> 00:17:29.460
It is not something
I think we should want.

00:17:29.460 --> 00:17:32.060
It is not something
that the Ukrainians will recognize.

00:17:32.100 --> 00:17:35.540
It is not something Ukrainian friends
and allies will recognize.

00:17:35.580 --> 00:17:39.660
But, Helen, you and I know
that there are many things in the world

00:17:39.700 --> 00:17:41.540
that we want that are not so.

00:17:41.540 --> 00:17:44.460
And it turns out that we live with them
for a very long time.

00:17:44.500 --> 00:17:45.780
I mean, the North Koreans,

00:17:45.820 --> 00:17:48.100
we do not accept
that they have nuclear weapons.

00:17:48.100 --> 00:17:50.180
They don't really care if we accept it.

00:17:50.220 --> 00:17:52.300
They have nuclear weapons.

00:17:52.300 --> 00:17:54.500
Denuclearization is not happening, right?

00:17:54.540 --> 00:17:56.100
So that's a reality.

00:17:56.540 --> 00:18:00.180
Ukraine is presently partitioned.

00:18:00.500 --> 00:18:02.220
And their ability,

00:18:02.220 --> 00:18:05.100
as much as the Americans, in principle,

00:18:05.140 --> 00:18:08.340
would like them to be able
to take their land back,

00:18:08.340 --> 00:18:10.580
and they have every right
to be able to do so,

00:18:10.620 --> 00:18:14.100
the invasions in 2014

00:18:14.140 --> 00:18:18.100
and then in 2022, right,

00:18:18.140 --> 00:18:19.620
were illegal.

00:18:19.660 --> 00:18:25.500
The Ukrainians did nothing,
to, you know, force them.

00:18:26.740 --> 00:18:29.900
And yet they can't take their land back.

00:18:30.220 --> 00:18:35.620
The American ability
and willingness to continue to lead

00:18:35.660 --> 00:18:37.460
in providing military support

00:18:37.500 --> 00:18:41.340
that would allow the Ukrainians
to get the 18 percent of their land

00:18:41.380 --> 00:18:43.380
that is presently occupied by the Russians

00:18:43.420 --> 00:18:44.860
and has been for a year now,

00:18:44.900 --> 00:18:46.620
that just does not exist in 2024.

00:18:46.660 --> 00:18:50.580
And in fact, the bigger danger
is that the trajectory of this war

00:18:50.580 --> 00:18:52.100
is at a turning point.

00:18:52.500 --> 00:18:56.940
And that not only will the Ukrainians
not be able to get their land back,

00:18:56.940 --> 00:19:00.460
but that they might not have
the people to continue to fight

00:19:00.500 --> 00:19:02.460
to keep the land they presently have.

00:19:03.140 --> 00:19:06.620
And that their ability
to get ongoing support,

00:19:06.620 --> 00:19:08.620
especially from the Americans,

00:19:08.660 --> 00:19:12.020
when this is becoming
a political divide in the US,

00:19:12.060 --> 00:19:13.740
far away,

00:19:13.740 --> 00:19:16.620
Israel-Palestine -- higher priority,

00:19:16.660 --> 00:19:19.340
US elections -- higher priority.

00:19:19.380 --> 00:19:22.700
Trump, if he's elected, at least 50-50,

00:19:22.700 --> 00:19:24.860
I'd put it a little higher
than that, frankly,

00:19:24.900 --> 00:19:27.340
though, I don't have a lot
of confidence around it,

00:19:27.340 --> 00:19:29.660
would end support
to Zelensky, no question.

00:19:29.700 --> 00:19:35.940
This is going to make the Ukrainians
feel incredibly vulnerable

00:19:35.980 --> 00:19:37.780
and increasingly desperate.

00:19:38.380 --> 00:19:41.900
That is what we're looking at in 2024.

00:19:41.940 --> 00:19:46.620
And of course, that not only threatens
Ukraine's territorial integrity ongoing,

00:19:46.660 --> 00:19:48.580
and Zelensky as a leader,

00:19:48.620 --> 00:19:51.940
but it also threatens the integrity
of the transatlantic alliance,

00:19:51.980 --> 00:19:54.900
which had been getting stronger
over the last couple of years

00:19:54.940 --> 00:19:56.540
post Russian invasion,

00:19:56.580 --> 00:20:00.300
and threatens the integrity of NATO
as an ongoing alliance,

00:20:00.340 --> 00:20:06.300
the most important military alliance
on the planet today

00:20:06.340 --> 00:20:08.940
So there's a lot going on here.

00:20:09.460 --> 00:20:13.140
And presently, the outcomes
don't look great.

00:20:13.860 --> 00:20:16.300
HW: So you have an interesting
phrase in the report

00:20:16.300 --> 00:20:18.740
that stated "Ukraine is at risk of losing,

00:20:18.740 --> 00:20:20.460
Russia has no way to win."

00:20:20.500 --> 00:20:24.900
So what does that stalemate
actually look like in practical terms?

00:20:24.900 --> 00:20:26.940
IB: So Ukraine is at risk of losing.

00:20:26.940 --> 00:20:28.580
Ukraine doesn't have to lose.

00:20:28.620 --> 00:20:31.260
There are still outcomes
where the Ukrainians can win,

00:20:31.300 --> 00:20:33.460
even though they will be
de facto partitioned.

00:20:33.500 --> 00:20:36.300
Now, that may not sound easy,

00:20:36.340 --> 00:20:37.540
and it's not.

00:20:37.580 --> 00:20:40.380
But let me throw you out a scenario

00:20:40.380 --> 00:20:46.100
where the Americans are able to provide
another 20, 30 billion dollars this year,

00:20:46.140 --> 00:20:50.660
the Europeans continue to provide
significant economic support,

00:20:50.700 --> 00:20:53.020
harder with the Hungarians opposing,

00:20:53.020 --> 00:20:56.500
harder with the Germans
and their emergency budget situation,

00:20:56.500 --> 00:20:57.700
but it can happen.

00:20:57.740 --> 00:20:59.300
And some of that could be taken,

00:20:59.300 --> 00:21:02.540
some of the frozen assets,
legally problematic, of Russia

00:21:02.540 --> 00:21:05.140
and applying them
to Ukrainian reconstruction.

00:21:05.140 --> 00:21:08.700
On top of that, you fast-track

00:21:08.740 --> 00:21:12.220
EU integration and reform in Ukraine,

00:21:12.260 --> 00:21:14.540
and you fast-track NATO membership,

00:21:14.540 --> 00:21:16.540
which cannot happen tomorrow.

00:21:16.540 --> 00:21:20.220
But it could happen within, you know,
say, you get all the countries together,

00:21:20.260 --> 00:21:22.780
the NATO allies, and say,
within two, three years

00:21:22.780 --> 00:21:25.620
you're going to provide
them NATO membership.

00:21:25.660 --> 00:21:28.860
It will not include territory
that the Russians are occupying

00:21:28.900 --> 00:21:32.020
because you're not going to go
to war directly with the Russians,

00:21:32.020 --> 00:21:34.740
but it will mean that these countries
will defend Ukraine

00:21:34.780 --> 00:21:36.100
in the remaining territory.

00:21:36.140 --> 00:21:37.820
That is a narrow path.

00:21:38.500 --> 00:21:40.140
But if it happens,

00:21:40.180 --> 00:21:43.340
you will have 80 percent
of Ukrainian territory

00:21:43.380 --> 00:21:46.460
that has a far better trajectory
for their people

00:21:46.460 --> 00:21:49.300
than Ukraine ever could have imagined

00:21:49.300 --> 00:21:50.940
had the Russians not invaded.

00:21:50.980 --> 00:21:54.500
None of that, none of that will make good

00:21:54.540 --> 00:21:56.860
the eight million people
who have been displaced,

00:21:56.900 --> 00:22:00.540
the tens of thousands that have had
war crimes committed against them,

00:22:00.540 --> 00:22:03.380
the children that have been abducted
and forced into Russia

00:22:03.420 --> 00:22:04.740
and on and on and on.

00:22:04.780 --> 00:22:08.540
I am not minimizing the crimes
that have been committed

00:22:08.540 --> 00:22:11.380
that they will remember
for generations in Ukraine.

00:22:11.420 --> 00:22:14.580
But I'm saying that Ukraine
still has a path to win,

00:22:14.580 --> 00:22:17.940
given what their country
looked like and was facing

00:22:17.980 --> 00:22:20.140
before 2022

00:22:20.180 --> 00:22:21.660
or before 2014.

00:22:22.060 --> 00:22:26.900
But irrespective of whether Ukraine
can accomplish that or will lose,

00:22:26.940 --> 00:22:28.460
the Russians will not win.

00:22:29.220 --> 00:22:30.540
And what I mean by that

00:22:30.580 --> 00:22:37.140
is Russia absolutely will be able
to maintain control

00:22:37.140 --> 00:22:39.860
of a strip of Ukrainian territory.

00:22:39.900 --> 00:22:41.860
Bombed out, right?

00:22:42.340 --> 00:22:44.380
And not productive,

00:22:44.420 --> 00:22:49.020
and years, if not decades,
to turn that land around economically.

00:22:49.900 --> 00:22:53.500
But Russia will now be facing
the rest of Ukraine

00:22:53.540 --> 00:22:58.460
with a visceral hatred for all things
Russia for generations.

00:22:58.460 --> 00:22:59.940
I mean, think about, you know,

00:22:59.940 --> 00:23:02.580
sort of the Turkish genocide
against the Armenians

00:23:02.620 --> 00:23:07.100
and for how long that drove
this historic enmity.

00:23:07.140 --> 00:23:08.700
That's what we're talking about.

00:23:08.700 --> 00:23:11.340
This is like, you know,
Hutu-Tutsi stuff, right,

00:23:11.340 --> 00:23:13.100
in Rwanda and Burundi,

00:23:13.140 --> 00:23:14.740
That's what you're talking about.

00:23:14.740 --> 00:23:19.100
And you have an expanded NATO,
including Finland,

00:23:19.140 --> 00:23:21.660
far more territory for the Russians
to have to defend,

00:23:21.660 --> 00:23:23.580
which ostensibly was the reason

00:23:23.620 --> 00:23:26.180
that the Russians went to war
against Ukraine,

00:23:26.220 --> 00:23:28.780
is because they didn't want
NATO to be encroaching.

00:23:28.780 --> 00:23:30.780
Well they're encroaching a lot more now.

00:23:30.820 --> 00:23:33.140
Hundreds of billions of dollars
of Russian assets

00:23:33.180 --> 00:23:34.820
that they will not have access to.

00:23:34.860 --> 00:23:37.340
Trade of Russia with the Europeans,

00:23:37.380 --> 00:23:39.820
with the United States -- no more, right?

00:23:39.820 --> 00:23:41.140
I mean, the Germans,

00:23:41.140 --> 00:23:46.900
much more quickly moving
to a post-carbon energy transition

00:23:46.940 --> 00:23:48.900
with no more Russian gas,

00:23:48.940 --> 00:23:50.580
because they cut the Russians off,

00:23:50.620 --> 00:23:52.500
at enormous cost to Germany,

00:23:52.500 --> 00:23:54.900
but greater cost long-term
to the Russians.

00:23:54.900 --> 00:23:58.540
Not to mention the million-plus
Russian civilians

00:23:58.580 --> 00:24:02.260
who have fled: young men capable, smart,

00:24:02.300 --> 00:24:06.620
that got out of dodge to anywhere,
to the Emirates, to Georgia,

00:24:06.660 --> 00:24:10.460
to anywhere that would accept them
so they wouldn't have to fight.

00:24:10.460 --> 00:24:14.340
And those are people that you could have
used productively in the Russian economy.

00:24:14.380 --> 00:24:16.220
So, I mean, in this environment,

00:24:16.260 --> 00:24:20.900
where Russia's only true friends,
providing military support for them,

00:24:20.940 --> 00:24:25.860
are Belarus, North Korea, Iran, right,

00:24:25.900 --> 00:24:29.460
I mean, this is -- with friends
like those, right?

00:24:29.500 --> 00:24:31.580
I mean, there's no way.

00:24:31.620 --> 00:24:34.540
I understand that if you're a tanky,

00:24:34.580 --> 00:24:35.740
as they call it,

00:24:35.780 --> 00:24:37.540
or if you're supported by the Kremlin

00:24:37.540 --> 00:24:40.180
and you're posting dutifully on Twitter/X

00:24:40.220 --> 00:24:43.460
that you will oppose
everything I just said

00:24:43.500 --> 00:24:45.100
because it's your job.

00:24:45.140 --> 00:24:48.100
But I mean, for those of us
that take a blue sky approach,

00:24:48.100 --> 00:24:51.540
which is that we look at it
and we kind of understand the science,

00:24:51.540 --> 00:24:54.100
it's impossible to say the Russians
are winning here.

00:24:54.940 --> 00:24:59.340
And that's a great cost
to the rest of the world,

00:24:59.380 --> 00:25:00.580
to the rest of the world.

00:25:00.620 --> 00:25:05.500
This was the worst
misjudgment, in my view,

00:25:05.500 --> 00:25:09.020
of any major leader on the global
stage since the Wall came down,

00:25:09.060 --> 00:25:13.540
was Putin's decision to invade
Ukraine in 2022.

00:25:13.580 --> 00:25:15.500
And not only was it a horrible decision,

00:25:15.500 --> 00:25:18.540
it was a horrible decision
that was facilitated by the Americans

00:25:18.580 --> 00:25:19.740
and the Europeans,

00:25:19.780 --> 00:25:24.020
who, after the 2014 invasion,
much smaller, didn't do anything.

00:25:24.020 --> 00:25:26.620
In fact, they kept doing business
with Russia as usual.

00:25:26.660 --> 00:25:29.900
They hosted the World Cup,
president said, "OK, we'll still go over."

00:25:29.900 --> 00:25:33.340
I mean, there were lots of signs
that Putin got that said,

00:25:33.380 --> 00:25:34.980
"These guys don't care.

00:25:34.980 --> 00:25:36.820
So we're going to get away with this."

00:25:36.820 --> 00:25:40.620
And unfortunately,
they were really, really wrong.

00:25:42.060 --> 00:25:44.460
HW: All right, let's change
subject a little bit.

00:25:44.500 --> 00:25:46.740
We mentioned artificial
intelligence earlier.

00:25:46.740 --> 00:25:51.620
And obviously, 2023 was the year
in which generative AI went mainstream.

00:25:51.660 --> 00:25:53.580
So it's exciting, and it's mind-blowing.

00:25:53.580 --> 00:25:56.060
And it's also led to something of a schism

00:25:56.060 --> 00:26:00.340
between some people who are super excited
about its creative potential

00:26:00.340 --> 00:26:02.900
and then others who can't believe
that we're barreling

00:26:02.900 --> 00:26:06.180
towards existential end of humanity
with such nonchalance.

00:26:06.180 --> 00:26:08.980
So you just have to see
the management drama at OpenAI

00:26:09.020 --> 00:26:12.580
that happened at the end of 2023
to understand some of the stakes here.

00:26:12.620 --> 00:26:14.740
But this isn't just about Silicon Valley,

00:26:14.780 --> 00:26:17.020
and I think it's important
that we all recognize

00:26:17.060 --> 00:26:19.300
that this is actually
going to affect all of us.

00:26:19.340 --> 00:26:21.420
So what do you think
we should be focused on

00:26:21.420 --> 00:26:24.380
when it comes to AI,
and what are we going to see in 2024?

00:26:24.700 --> 00:26:27.340
IB: I am both of those things.

00:26:27.380 --> 00:26:30.700
I'm an enormous enthusiast about AI.

00:26:30.700 --> 00:26:36.180
I think it's a transformative technology
for everyone that has access to it,

00:26:36.180 --> 00:26:39.580
and we're just seeing
the beginnings of that.

00:26:40.460 --> 00:26:45.340
One of the reasons I'm most excited
about AI is because it is transformative

00:26:45.340 --> 00:26:51.620
not just in displacing existing
powerful people and institutions.

00:26:51.660 --> 00:26:54.740
So climate change, people get very
excited about the transition.

00:26:54.740 --> 00:26:57.100
But to do that transition,
you've got to end oil.

00:26:57.100 --> 00:27:00.580
And so fossil fuel players
and those that are attached to them,

00:27:00.620 --> 00:27:03.900
infrastructure, transport
and the rest, have a lot to lose.

00:27:03.940 --> 00:27:06.940
AI, like, even if you're a coal miner,

00:27:06.940 --> 00:27:10.780
you can and will use AI to be
much more effective at mining coal.

00:27:10.820 --> 00:27:14.100
You know, AI, you're like
a traditional airline company.

00:27:14.140 --> 00:27:18.540
You can use AI to reduce, you know,
energy intensity in the contrails.

00:27:18.540 --> 00:27:20.460
You're going to do that tomorrow, right?

00:27:20.500 --> 00:27:25.140
So it's astonishing how much uplift
we will get from productivity

00:27:25.180 --> 00:27:27.300
and efficiency in every sector,

00:27:27.340 --> 00:27:30.860
from all sorts of corporations
and from individual workers,

00:27:30.860 --> 00:27:33.100
by using these tools,
by deploying these tools.

00:27:33.140 --> 00:27:38.540
So I'm very excited about how much
we will get out of AI

00:27:38.580 --> 00:27:41.380
to unlock human potential
broader and faster.

00:27:42.220 --> 00:27:46.180
But I don't spend much time
talking about that, usually,

00:27:46.220 --> 00:27:48.940
because you've got a lot
of people and companies

00:27:48.980 --> 00:27:52.140
that are worth trillions
of dollars collectively

00:27:52.140 --> 00:27:54.300
that are spending
all of their time and effort

00:27:54.340 --> 00:27:56.220
doing that as fast as humanly possible.

00:27:56.260 --> 00:27:58.220
And they have to, because if they don't,

00:27:58.220 --> 00:28:01.820
they've got very smart and well bankrolled
people breathing down their neck

00:28:01.860 --> 00:28:05.020
that would be very happy
to displace them, right?

00:28:05.020 --> 00:28:07.620
That's the principle AI displacement
we're going to see

00:28:07.660 --> 00:28:08.860
in the next 24 months,

00:28:08.860 --> 00:28:12.140
will be of AI players
that get taken out by other AI players

00:28:12.140 --> 00:28:14.260
because it's so cutthroat and fast.

00:28:14.820 --> 00:28:18.740
But there aren't people
that are spending their time thinking

00:28:18.780 --> 00:28:24.500
about what are the challenges
for the common good.

00:28:25.140 --> 00:28:30.420
What are the negative externalities
that come from AI?

00:28:30.420 --> 00:28:33.860
I mean, so you think
about the last industrialization

00:28:33.900 --> 00:28:36.460
and Americans in particular, my country,

00:28:36.500 --> 00:28:41.380
we are so great at the private market
when it comes to profits.

00:28:41.380 --> 00:28:43.740
We're capitalists when
it comes to the markets,

00:28:43.780 --> 00:28:46.260
because when we do well
and money is to be made,

00:28:46.300 --> 00:28:47.860
we make sure that we deploy that

00:28:47.900 --> 00:28:50.380
and the shareholders get it,
and we're focused.

00:28:50.420 --> 00:28:53.860
But when there are losses,
we are the world's best socialists.

00:28:54.220 --> 00:28:55.820
It's not us.

00:28:55.860 --> 00:28:57.140
It's like, somebody else.

00:28:57.180 --> 00:28:58.980
Everyone's got to pay for it.

00:28:58.980 --> 00:29:00.140
We're not responsible.

00:29:00.180 --> 00:29:01.340
Preferably the kids.

00:29:01.380 --> 00:29:03.460
And maybe not now, but maybe later.

00:29:03.460 --> 00:29:06.020
And we've seen that with climate change.

00:29:06.020 --> 00:29:08.700
Very happy to make all the money
from industrialization.

00:29:08.740 --> 00:29:10.860
But I mean the costs
of emitting more carbon?

00:29:10.860 --> 00:29:12.220
Not it, not our problem.

00:29:12.260 --> 00:29:17.100
Well, that's a long, global, slow
process over generations.

00:29:17.700 --> 00:29:23.060
AI is a very fast, transformative
process over like, five to 10 years.

00:29:23.420 --> 00:29:29.420
And the negative externalities are going
to happen essentially simultaneously

00:29:29.420 --> 00:29:34.140
with all of the positive
productivity and efficiency gains.

00:29:34.580 --> 00:29:38.380
And we need a governance environment

00:29:38.380 --> 00:29:41.300
where those are accounted for

00:29:41.340 --> 00:29:42.580
and paid for.

00:29:42.620 --> 00:29:44.460
And in the near-term,

00:29:44.500 --> 00:29:47.580
I am not talking
about the robots taking over,

00:29:47.620 --> 00:29:50.780
existential risk of artificial
general intelligence.

00:29:50.780 --> 00:29:55.580
I'm talking about AI
being used by bad actors

00:29:55.620 --> 00:29:59.660
or indifferent actors
with challenging business models

00:29:59.700 --> 00:30:04.220
in ways that will undermine
stable society.

00:30:04.980 --> 00:30:08.340
I'm talking about,
in particular, disinformation

00:30:08.380 --> 00:30:11.620
and how it can be deployed
to undermine democracy.

00:30:12.060 --> 00:30:14.140
And I'm also talking

00:30:14.140 --> 00:30:18.940
about the disruptive nature
of proliferated AI tools

00:30:18.980 --> 00:30:22.020
in the hands of large
numbers of governments

00:30:22.020 --> 00:30:24.540
and institutions and individuals

00:30:24.580 --> 00:30:27.420
that are bad actors or tinkerers
that want to destroy things

00:30:27.420 --> 00:30:28.900
or thinking about it.

00:30:28.940 --> 00:30:31.700
I mean, whether they're using
AI for malware

00:30:31.740 --> 00:30:34.940
or they're using AI to build viruses

00:30:34.980 --> 00:30:36.860
or lethal autonomous weapons.

00:30:36.860 --> 00:30:41.300
And I think that that is really becoming
a risk only for the first time

00:30:41.300 --> 00:30:42.860
in the coming year.

00:30:43.500 --> 00:30:46.020
In elections, like the US election,

00:30:46.060 --> 00:30:48.780
especially as the next generation
of AI comes out

00:30:48.820 --> 00:30:51.420
and this is moving three times
faster than Moore's Law.

00:30:51.460 --> 00:30:56.180
So every six months you're getting
a doubling capacity, which is, you know,

00:30:56.220 --> 00:30:59.580
not an environment that traditional
governments are able to respond to.

00:30:59.620 --> 00:31:01.420
It's too fast, it's too powerful.

00:31:01.820 --> 00:31:04.260
And you're also getting those tools

00:31:04.300 --> 00:31:09.180
in the hands of hundreds of millions
of people in very, very short order.

00:31:09.220 --> 00:31:14.380
So there is governance,
it is coming fast, it is urgent.

00:31:14.380 --> 00:31:18.980
But in 2024, the speed of the technology
is much, much faster

00:31:19.020 --> 00:31:20.980
than the speed of the governance.

00:31:21.020 --> 00:31:24.260
And that gap will create crises.

00:31:25.060 --> 00:31:26.460
HW: I mean, we've seen,

00:31:26.500 --> 00:31:30.140
from the whole of the OpenAI kerfuffle
that happened at the end of the year,

00:31:30.180 --> 00:31:33.140
you really see these tensions
kind of playing out in real time.

00:31:33.180 --> 00:31:35.340
And there are people
within large corporations

00:31:35.340 --> 00:31:37.660
who are trying to do
the right thing internally,

00:31:37.660 --> 00:31:40.340
but then, of course, competition
is actually driving them

00:31:40.380 --> 00:31:43.260
to move faster and faster
so that they don't get left behind.

00:31:43.300 --> 00:31:44.860
Given the fact that, as you say,

00:31:44.860 --> 00:31:47.540
there are these bad actors
outside of these organizations

00:31:47.580 --> 00:31:50.940
who are also trying to do their thing
with the open-source technologies

00:31:50.980 --> 00:31:52.380
that are being released, etc.

00:31:52.420 --> 00:31:55.980
What actual governance is possible
in that environment?

00:31:55.980 --> 00:31:57.820
IB: There's a lot of governance

00:31:57.860 --> 00:31:59.660
that is possible in that environment,

00:31:59.700 --> 00:32:02.660
but it's not clear how quickly
we can get there.

00:32:02.700 --> 00:32:06.580
So I mean, the EU has their AI Act,

00:32:06.580 --> 00:32:10.940
which is quite comprehensive

00:32:10.980 --> 00:32:15.820
in the way it thinks about regulation
and transparency of foundational models

00:32:15.860 --> 00:32:18.260
and testing of those models of AI.

00:32:18.780 --> 00:32:22.020
And in making sure it's not
just being done internally.

00:32:22.620 --> 00:32:26.220
And in watermarking
and trying to make sure

00:32:26.260 --> 00:32:30.700
that people are aware of what kind
of images they're seeing

00:32:30.740 --> 00:32:34.620
and audio, and what's being driven by AI

00:32:34.660 --> 00:32:36.020
and what is not.

00:32:36.620 --> 00:32:40.180
And also the deployment of AI models

00:32:40.220 --> 00:32:43.260
and what kind of data they have access to,

00:32:43.300 --> 00:32:44.460
all of those things.

00:32:44.460 --> 00:32:49.660
And the US has an executive order,
which is not as powerful as legislation,

00:32:49.700 --> 00:32:51.980
which is not coming anytime soon,

00:32:52.020 --> 00:32:54.180
given the divided nature
of the US government.

00:32:54.180 --> 00:32:56.620
But still, you know,
represents a step change

00:32:56.660 --> 00:33:00.500
in how you think about regulating
these technologies.

00:33:00.540 --> 00:33:04.380
But these processes
are happening much more slowly

00:33:04.420 --> 00:33:05.820
than the tech is rolling out.

00:33:05.860 --> 00:33:08.220
So I think what governance will get you

00:33:08.260 --> 00:33:12.780
is it will identify the actors
that are critical,

00:33:12.780 --> 00:33:15.820
both inside governments
and in the private sector.

00:33:15.820 --> 00:33:19.580
It will have them talking
about the issues that really matter,

00:33:19.580 --> 00:33:26.060
and better set up so that when
a crisis occurs, and it will,

00:33:26.060 --> 00:33:30.020
that they will be able to identify
and respond to it collectively

00:33:30.060 --> 00:33:34.060
and much more quickly than they would
absent that governance structure.

00:33:34.060 --> 00:33:35.740
So in this regard,

00:33:35.740 --> 00:33:39.060
AI is a little bit more
like the financial sector,

00:33:39.100 --> 00:33:42.340
where we all know that we need
a functioning financial sector.

00:33:42.380 --> 00:33:45.940
And that's true whether
we're capitalists like in the US

00:33:45.940 --> 00:33:50.300
or we're state capitalists, like in China,
you got a free, you know,

00:33:50.340 --> 00:33:53.460
sort of, convertible currency
or you've got a closed market.

00:33:53.500 --> 00:33:54.660
Doesn't matter.

00:33:54.660 --> 00:33:56.540
We all know we need a financial market.

00:33:56.540 --> 00:34:00.380
But we also know that in a global
financial market,

00:34:00.420 --> 00:34:04.180
that individual actors
can cause systemic crises.

00:34:04.220 --> 00:34:10.780
And so we need a stability board,
that when there is a crisis,

00:34:10.780 --> 00:34:13.500
everyone identifies it
and responds immediately

00:34:13.500 --> 00:34:15.580
so that we don't have a global meltdown,

00:34:15.620 --> 00:34:17.100
don't face a depression.

00:34:17.100 --> 00:34:22.100
That's what governance is going to need
to be able to do on the AI front.

00:34:22.140 --> 00:34:25.340
And the thing is about AI,
we're not just talking about,

00:34:25.340 --> 00:34:27.260
oh, someone's making a run on the market

00:34:27.300 --> 00:34:29.300
and this company is going to go bankrupt.

00:34:29.300 --> 00:34:34.260
The risks that potentially come
from AI and misuse of AI

00:34:34.260 --> 00:34:38.100
are in many, many different types
of technologies and applications.

00:34:38.140 --> 00:34:40.660
So you're going to have to,

00:34:40.700 --> 00:34:45.100
you're going to have to essentially
build the ship as you're steering it,

00:34:45.140 --> 00:34:48.100
because the dangers
are going to change very quickly.

00:34:49.220 --> 00:34:51.140
HW: No problem, we'll do that just fine.

00:34:51.180 --> 00:34:54.460
So there is another part
to the tech challenge of 2024

00:34:54.500 --> 00:34:56.660
that is actually around
the building of tech.

00:34:56.660 --> 00:34:59.140
And that's about the minerals
and the materials

00:34:59.140 --> 00:35:01.380
that actually go into building tech.

00:35:01.860 --> 00:35:04.140
So your hunch within this report

00:35:04.180 --> 00:35:06.620
is that governments
are going to turn protectionist,

00:35:06.660 --> 00:35:08.780
they're going to disrupt
the flow of minerals

00:35:08.780 --> 00:35:11.020
that are needed to build
all of this technology.

00:35:11.060 --> 00:35:13.220
So what should we be paying
attention to here,

00:35:13.260 --> 00:35:16.700
and how much of a risk do you think
that this is for the future?

00:35:16.740 --> 00:35:20.340
IB: Well, one is, it's related
to the high-tech front,

00:35:20.380 --> 00:35:24.020
which is that the biggest fight
between the US and China,

00:35:24.020 --> 00:35:26.780
which thankfully has a more stable

00:35:26.820 --> 00:35:30.020
and more managed relationship in 2024

00:35:30.060 --> 00:35:32.820
than they did coming into 2023.

00:35:32.860 --> 00:35:36.940
One of the big areas of tension
is on the high-tech side.

00:35:36.980 --> 00:35:39.780
And the Chinese are trying to develop AI,

00:35:39.820 --> 00:35:41.940
the Americans are trying to develop AI,

00:35:41.940 --> 00:35:44.780
but the US, with their allies,

00:35:44.780 --> 00:35:49.540
are now taking semiconductor capabilities

00:35:49.580 --> 00:35:54.420
and trying to ensure that they
are being built in trusted countries,

00:35:54.460 --> 00:35:57.700
and they're putting export controls
on semiconductors,

00:35:57.740 --> 00:36:00.860
cloud computing, related infrastructure.

00:36:01.380 --> 00:36:05.300
And so the Chinese don't have access
to the most valuable stuff.

00:36:05.700 --> 00:36:10.620
Now, China doesn't build its own
high-tech semiconductors.

00:36:10.660 --> 00:36:14.780
And they are, you know,
by most accounts, about 10 years behind.

00:36:14.820 --> 00:36:17.380
And they don't have a really
good way to counter that,

00:36:17.380 --> 00:36:21.300
aside from investing as much as they can
inside their own country to rebuild it.

00:36:21.300 --> 00:36:23.340
So some of what they're trying to do

00:36:23.340 --> 00:36:26.420
is see if there's a way
to engage with the Americans

00:36:26.460 --> 00:36:29.740
that might loosen some
of those export controls.

00:36:29.780 --> 00:36:32.700
And that's one of the reasons,
the principal reason, actually,

00:36:32.740 --> 00:36:37.540
why China expressed a willingness
to join a new Track 1.5 dialogue

00:36:37.540 --> 00:36:39.940
with the Americans
on artificial intelligence.

00:36:40.340 --> 00:36:45.580
But, the Chinese response has been,

00:36:45.620 --> 00:36:48.380
well, you guys are dominant
in semiconductors,

00:36:48.420 --> 00:36:51.500
but we're dominant in critical minerals,

00:36:51.500 --> 00:36:55.220
in the exploitation
and in the supply chain.

00:36:55.260 --> 00:36:57.940
And also in the development of a lot

00:36:57.980 --> 00:37:03.940
of the new post-carbon energy
generation and infrastructure:

00:37:03.980 --> 00:37:07.980
solar cells, wind, batteries,
that kind of thing,

00:37:08.020 --> 00:37:09.660
electric automobiles.

00:37:09.700 --> 00:37:11.620
And so the Chinese are basically saying,

00:37:11.660 --> 00:37:17.780
we're going to look at some of those
critical minerals that we dominate,

00:37:17.780 --> 00:37:22.060
and we're going to put licensing
regimes on those.

00:37:22.100 --> 00:37:26.220
And if you stick with the problems
you give us on semis,

00:37:26.260 --> 00:37:28.660
we're going to start putting
export controls there.

00:37:28.660 --> 00:37:31.420
Now, the difference is
the Americans will still be able

00:37:31.460 --> 00:37:34.100
to buy all of that stuff.

00:37:34.100 --> 00:37:35.940
It will just be more expensive.

00:37:35.980 --> 00:37:39.060
So the Chinese can't cut off the Americans

00:37:39.100 --> 00:37:41.100
the way the Americans
and the South Koreans

00:37:41.140 --> 00:37:44.340
and the Dutch can cut off the Chinese,

00:37:44.340 --> 00:37:46.500
but they can make it really painful.

00:37:46.540 --> 00:37:49.820
And ultimately, all of this
is about relative power.

00:37:49.860 --> 00:37:51.620
All of this is about relative gains.

00:37:51.660 --> 00:37:53.100
It's not about like, you know,

00:37:53.100 --> 00:37:56.580
destroying, the Americans aren't going
to destroy China and vice versa.

00:37:57.300 --> 00:38:03.260
But it does mean that as the world
barrels towards an effort

00:38:03.260 --> 00:38:08.340
to transition from primarily
fossil fuel-developed energy

00:38:08.380 --> 00:38:11.780
to primarily post-carbon-developed energy

00:38:11.820 --> 00:38:14.620
over the period of one generation,

00:38:14.620 --> 00:38:17.460
that it will be far less efficient

00:38:17.500 --> 00:38:21.460
because of this big fight
between the United States and China.

00:38:21.460 --> 00:38:22.980
And at the same time,

00:38:23.020 --> 00:38:27.260
we are seeing a number
of governments around the world

00:38:27.260 --> 00:38:31.180
put industrial policies in place
to provide subsidies

00:38:31.180 --> 00:38:33.740
for their own workforces,

00:38:33.780 --> 00:38:37.900
governments that have access
to a lot of these critical minerals

00:38:37.900 --> 00:38:40.740
that are saying, we can use this
to get up the supply chain.

00:38:40.780 --> 00:38:43.860
So we want to make sure
that you're investing that new technology

00:38:43.860 --> 00:38:47.460
in our country and not just exploiting
us for our resources,

00:38:47.500 --> 00:38:52.100
all of which is creating significant,
politically induced costs

00:38:52.100 --> 00:38:55.700
in that big sector
of the global environment

00:38:55.740 --> 00:38:58.580
at a time that you want those costs
to be as low as possible

00:38:58.620 --> 00:39:00.980
so you can move away
from fossil fuels, right?

00:39:01.020 --> 00:39:04.060
I mean, the more expensive
the critical minerals are,

00:39:04.100 --> 00:39:05.900
the bigger the barriers,

00:39:05.900 --> 00:39:11.900
the harder it's going to be
to go from coal and oil and gas

00:39:11.940 --> 00:39:15.460
to all of this new sustainable stuff.

00:39:16.380 --> 00:39:19.380
In 2024, that's becoming
a significant fight.

00:39:20.740 --> 00:39:22.700
HW: Everything, everywhere, all at once.

00:39:22.740 --> 00:39:26.060
Everything is interconnected, is I think
a theme of this conversation.

00:39:26.100 --> 00:39:29.020
I will flag for everybody
that there are 10 risks

00:39:29.020 --> 00:39:30.580
that you are flagging this year.

00:39:30.580 --> 00:39:32.660
We have not had time
to go through them all,

00:39:32.700 --> 00:39:35.900
so I would highly recommend
that you go and read the full report.

00:39:35.940 --> 00:39:37.300
I'm going to, spoiler alert,

00:39:37.300 --> 00:39:40.660
just flag something that you wrote
right at the end of the report, Ian,

00:39:40.660 --> 00:39:44.260
that I think is really beautiful and worth
holding close as we go into 2024:

00:39:44.260 --> 00:39:47.540
"... that it is critical we don't just 
talk about these global issues

00:39:47.540 --> 00:39:49.540
to help make business
and policy decisions,

00:39:49.540 --> 00:39:51.780
but also to connect
to those closest to us.

00:39:51.820 --> 00:39:55.380
If we can't make a difference with those
we know and love, we are lost."

00:39:55.420 --> 00:39:57.780
Ian, you are a poet, it turns out.

00:39:58.980 --> 00:40:00.740
IB: I don't know if I'd go that far,

00:40:00.740 --> 00:40:03.700
but I'd like to believe I care
about my fellow people.

00:40:04.540 --> 00:40:06.020
And this is a tough year.

00:40:06.060 --> 00:40:10.780
Look, I mean, I just came back
from the South Pole, of all places.

00:40:10.820 --> 00:40:12.980
It's a place that I've
always wanted to visit.

00:40:14.980 --> 00:40:17.380
By virtue of my job,
I travel all around the world.

00:40:17.380 --> 00:40:20.340
And this is this massive continent
at the bottom of the planet

00:40:20.380 --> 00:40:21.580
that I've never been to.

00:40:21.620 --> 00:40:23.740
And it just looks like this
big thing of ice.

00:40:23.780 --> 00:40:26.780
But this also felt like a year
that being off the grid

00:40:26.820 --> 00:40:28.220
and connected to the planet,

00:40:28.260 --> 00:40:31.380
but not like in the headlines,
in the news, every moment.

00:40:31.420 --> 00:40:34.460
It was very useful just in terms
of clearing my head a bit.

00:40:34.500 --> 00:40:36.460
But also, as a political scientist,

00:40:36.460 --> 00:40:41.620
being in a place that for 60-some years
now we've managed to govern,

00:40:41.660 --> 00:40:43.580
if I can use that term loosely,

00:40:43.620 --> 00:40:46.300
in a way that protects it
for future generations

00:40:46.300 --> 00:40:47.500
and for the planet.

00:40:47.540 --> 00:40:50.980
And there used to be all of these
competing territorial claims

00:40:50.980 --> 00:40:52.580
by all these different countries.

00:40:52.620 --> 00:40:54.820
And now we actually have a treaty

00:40:54.860 --> 00:40:58.180
that says we're not going to have
any resource exploitation.

00:40:58.220 --> 00:41:00.620
We're not going to use this
for military bases,

00:41:00.660 --> 00:41:02.540
we're just going to use it for science,

00:41:02.540 --> 00:41:04.780
and we're just going
to keep it for the planet.

00:41:04.780 --> 00:41:07.380
We're melting it, I know,
because of global challenges.

00:41:07.380 --> 00:41:10.780
But the reality is it still kind of is
the way that we wanted it to be,

00:41:10.820 --> 00:41:13.780
was the first arms control agreement
that was ever signed

00:41:13.820 --> 00:41:16.900
between the Americans
and the Soviets back in 1959,

00:41:16.900 --> 00:41:20.380
and the treaty holds true until 2048.

00:41:20.380 --> 00:41:23.620
And when I talked about it,
when I just came back,

00:41:23.660 --> 00:41:26.540
I had some people that were saying,
oh, well, you know, 2048,

00:41:26.540 --> 00:41:29.780
the Chinese are going to be like,
looking on how they can exploit it,

00:41:29.820 --> 00:41:31.900
and the Russians, it's not going to last.

00:41:31.900 --> 00:41:34.460
It's like, you know, if we've
got something that works

00:41:34.460 --> 00:41:37.220
and is going to work until 2048
in today's environment,

00:41:37.260 --> 00:41:39.900
like, that's a win, right?

00:41:39.900 --> 00:41:43.340
I mean, we don't know where we're going
to be in three months right now.

00:41:43.380 --> 00:41:46.300
And it turns out that we as human beings

00:41:46.340 --> 00:41:49.220
are capable, sometimes,
when we put our minds to it

00:41:49.220 --> 00:41:50.740
of being stewards.

00:41:51.140 --> 00:41:53.860
And we need to do more of that.

00:41:53.900 --> 00:41:57.020
The thing that worries me
the most about 2024

00:41:57.020 --> 00:41:59.220
is we have a lot of very big,

00:41:59.260 --> 00:42:01.940
very real fights happening right now,

00:42:01.980 --> 00:42:04.180
and no one's acting like a leader.

00:42:04.180 --> 00:42:06.380
No one's acting like a steward.

00:42:06.420 --> 00:42:10.460
People are all focused
on a very narrow view

00:42:10.500 --> 00:42:12.540
of what's at stake for them

00:42:12.580 --> 00:42:14.540
and the people that like them.

00:42:14.580 --> 00:42:16.420
And that's not humanity, right?

00:42:16.460 --> 00:42:18.260
It's very ephemeral.

00:42:18.300 --> 00:42:21.340
And and when we all leave this planet,

00:42:21.380 --> 00:42:25.220
after this very short spell
that we have here,

00:42:25.220 --> 00:42:29.660
we all go back to the same place
where all the same atoms.

00:42:29.660 --> 00:42:33.940
And we need to take a little bit of time
to connect with the people around us,

00:42:33.940 --> 00:42:35.660
to remember that.

00:42:35.700 --> 00:42:38.100
That's what I took away most
from my little trip

00:42:38.140 --> 00:42:39.940
down to the bottom of the planet.

00:42:39.980 --> 00:42:44.300
And I'd like to try to hold
that with me as we get through 2024.

00:42:45.260 --> 00:42:46.700
HW: May we all borrow that,

00:42:46.700 --> 00:42:49.980
may we all have that sensibility in mind

00:42:49.980 --> 00:42:51.500
as we go through this year.

00:42:51.500 --> 00:42:53.300
Ian, thank you so much for your time.

00:42:53.300 --> 00:42:54.540
IB: Thank you, Helen.

